# Changelog

The admin Changelog page (`/dashboard/admin/changelog`) renders
`app/src/config/changelog/changelog.json`. There is no database and nothing
parses git history at build time, so that file is written by hand — by you,
right after a commit lands. This applies to **every** commit you make in this
repo, whether or not you got there through the `scoped-commit` skill.

Skip it entirely for: merge commits, a revert of your own commit from this
session, and the changelog bookkeeping commit itself.

## One entry per piece of work

Work arrives as one substantial request followed by refinements ("also add X",
"change Y", "fix that"). The whole arc is **one** changelog entry — not one per
commit.

- **First commit of a new piece of work → append a new entry.**
- **Every later commit that refines, extends, fixes or polishes that same work
  → update the entry you already wrote.** Never append a second entry for it.

It is a refinement when it builds on work you already logged in this session.
It is new work when the user moves to a different feature or area of the app.
When it is genuinely unclear, **prefer updating**: one accurate entry per
feature reads better than three entries narrating the same feature in stages.

When updating an existing entry:

- keep `commit` pointing at the **first** commit of that work — it is the
  entry's stable anchor, and rewriting it breaks the link an admin already saw
- set `date` to the latest commit's date
- rewrite `title` and `description` so they describe the finished thing, not
  its first draft — the entry is a record of what shipped, not of what you did
  first
- refresh `sessionMood` / `sessionNote` to cover the session so far
- commit the file alone with `Update the <short title> changelog entry` and a
  `Changelog: skip` trailer

## The entry

Append one object to the **end** of the array. Never reorder, reformat, or
rewrite entries other than the one you are updating. Keep the file's 2-space
indent and its trailing newline.

```json
{
  "commit": "<%h>",
  "date": "<%ad>",
  "type": "FEATURE",
  "title": "<the commit subject, verbatim>",
  "description": "One to three plain sentences an admin can read: what changed and why it matters. Not the raw commit body.",
  "author": "<%an>",
  "agent": "Claude Opus 5 (1M context)",
  "sessionMood": "FOCUSED",
  "sessionNote": "One factual sentence about how the session ran."
}
```

`type` is one of:

- `FEATURE` — a new user-visible capability
- `IMPROVEMENT` — existing behaviour made better (UX, copy, performance)
- `FIX` — a bug fix
- `CHORE` — deps, tooling, docs, internal refactor with no user-visible effect

`agent` is your own model name, matching the `Co-Authored-By` trailer on the
commit (`null` when there is none).

`sessionMood` is one of `HAPPY` (upbeat, quick approvals), `FOCUSED` (knew
exactly what they wanted), `RUSHED` (short messages, wanted it fast),
`EXPLANATORY` (gave lots of context up front), `EXPLORATORY` (figured it out as
we went), `FRUSTRATED` (repeated corrections, or things kept going wrong), or
`BLUNT` (curt or harsh tone, little context). `sessionNote` is one sentence
about it:

- Judge only from what is observable in the session — message length and pace,
  how much context was given, re-dos and corrections, stated approval or
  annoyance. Not inferred feelings, not traits, not psychoanalysis.
- Keep it factual and specific ("short, direct asks, no back-and-forth"), never
  an insult and never a verdict on the person.
- Describe the session, not the human: "the session ran hot after the third
  failed attempt", not "he was angry".
- Do not quote anything sensitive the user pasted.
- **Omit both fields** when the session is too short or too mixed to call. They
  are optional, and a wrong label is worse than none.

## Mechanics

A commit cannot contain its own hash, and amending would change it, so the
entry always lands in a second, tiny commit:

1. Commit the change itself.
2. Read it: `git log -1 --format='%h|%ad|%an' --date=short`.
3. Append the new entry, or update the one you wrote earlier in this session.
4. `git add app/src/config/changelog/changelog.json` and commit that file alone,
   with a `Changelog: skip` trailer and **no** `Co-Authored-By` trailer —
   entries are selected by that co-author trailer, so leaving it off keeps the
   bookkeeping commit out of the changelog it just wrote.

## Rules

- If `changelog.json` has uncommitted edits you did not make, stop and flag it
  instead of writing on top of someone else's work.
- Never use `app/scripts/backfill-changelog.mjs` to add or update an entry. It
  rebuilds the whole file from git history and drops hand-written descriptions
  and every session mood/note; it is a one-off repair tool.
